# Cloudflare response headers. Pages and Workers Static Assets parse the same
# format; `wrangler.jsonc` deploys this build to Workers.
#
# The file is copied into the build output (`dist/_headers`) and applied to
# static asset responses. It is not served as a file itself.
#
# The security headers apply to everything. Cloudflare joins the headers of
# every matching rule, so a catch-all that also set Cache-Control would join
# with the /assets/* rule and turn "immutable" into "immutable, no-cache" --
# the worst of both. That is what the `!` detaches below are for: the broad rule
# sets the document policy, and each narrower rule drops it before setting its
# own value, so two Cache-Control values are never joined. Rules are applied in
# file order, which is why every detach sits after the rule it overrides.
#
# Two facts drive the cache policy:
#
#  1. Vite emits content-hashed filenames under /assets, so the bytes behind a
#     URL never change. A year of immutable caching is why a deploy is cheap
#     for a returning visitor.
#  2. Everything else -- the HTML shell, the prerendered route documents, the
#     service worker, the manifest -- is revalidated. A cached shell is what
#     strands a visitor on a build whose hashed chunks are gone, and PLAN.md
#     34.7 shipped recovery logic for exactly that.
#
# `no-transform` on the catch-all is not a cache setting. Cloudflare rewrites
# HTML it is allowed to modify, and one of the things it injects is its own Web
# Analytics beacon (static.cloudflareinsights.com/beacon.min.js). This product's
# promise is that there is no analytics and no third-party script, so the CSP in
# apps/web/src/lib/csp.ts blocks that beacon -- which leaves a CSP violation in
# every visitor's console, and makes the policy the only thing standing between
# the claim and the edge. `no-transform` stops the rewrite at the source:
# Cloudflare may not modify a payload it is told not to transform. It sits on
# the catch-all rather than on each prerendered route because an unmatched path
# is served `index.html` as the SPA fallback, and that document must be covered
# too.
#
# The cost is Cloudflare's edge compression of HTML, which it skips for a
# `no-transform` payload. The documents are ~16 kB; every hashed asset is
# detached below and still compresses normally.
#
#   pnpm --filter @pdfshush/web build
#   curl -sI https://pdfshush.space/ | grep -i cache-control
#   -> public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, no-transform
#
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/web-analytics/faq/
# https://developers.cloudflare.com/cache/concepts/cache-control/

/*
	X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
	Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
	X-Frame-Options: DENY
	Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate, no-transform

/assets/*
	! Cache-Control
	Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/robots.txt
	! Cache-Control
	Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

/sitemap.xml
	! Cache-Control
	Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600

# The Content-Security-Policy is a meta tag in the document itself
# (apps/web/src/lib/csp.ts), so it needs no header here.
